  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}-4y=f(x),\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)\u223c$  \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(n\\pi x))$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{\u2032}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{\u2032}-4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{\u2032}-4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)\u223cA{e}^{4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=5.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{4(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 5 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=5{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)\u223c5{e}^{4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(n\\pi x)).\n$$",
